(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-pathophysiology-i-exam-2-cardiac-pathophysiology-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Cardiac Pathophysiology Quiz 2 — Clinical Pathophysiology I","category":"Clinical Pathophysiology I","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"What defines coronary heart disease?","choices":["Failure of the myocardium to contract because of intrinsic muscle disease","Inflammation of the coronary arteries with leukocyte infiltration","Obstruction of venous drainage from the myocardium","Insufficient delivery of oxygenated blood to the myocardium because of atherosclerotic vessels"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The definition is about supply failing rather than about pain or any particular syndrome, which is why so many different presentations sit underneath it. — 6. Cardiac Pathophysiology for posting.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"What sequence follows the raised left atrial pressure of mitral stenosis?","choices":["Left ventricular hypertrophy, then left sided failure","Aortic root dilation","Atrial enlargement and hypertrophy","Pericardial effusion and tamponade"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A valve on the left side of the heart ends by failing the right side, because the pressure has nowhere to go but backwards through the lungs. Raised pulmonary pressures, then right sided hypertrophy and failure. — 6. Cardiac Pathophysiology for posting.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"What pattern of chamber involvement occurs in myocarditis?","choices":["Left ventricular dysfunction with general dilation of all four chambers","Isolated right ventricular hypertrophy","Left atrial enlargement alone","No chamber change, with inflammation confined to the endocardium"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The dysfunction is focused on the left ventricle while the dilation is general, which is a combination worth holding. — 6. Cardiac Pathophysiology for posting.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"What is the central haemodynamic abnormality in mitral stenosis?","choices":["An abnormal gradient during systole","An abnormal left atrial to left ventricular pressure gradient during diastole","Backflow from the ventricle into the atrium during systole","Obstruction to outflow from the left ventricle"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The atrium stays at higher pressure than the ventricle through diastole, and the gradient widens as the stenosis worsens. — 6. Cardiac Pathophysiology for posting.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"How does pericardial disease usually arise?","choices":["As a primary condition in most cases","Only as a consequence of myocardial infarction","Secondary to another primary cause","Only from direct infection of the pericardium"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. It is usually a manifestation of something else, and whatever the cause, fluid accumulates and the pericardial structures become painfully inflamed. Rarely as a primary condition. — 6. Cardiac Pathophysiology for posting.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"How does an infarcted area change over the weeks that follow?","choices":["It is replaced by functioning muscle within two weeks","Pale within a day","It calcifies within forty-eight hours","It remains unchanged indefinitely"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The sequence runs from removal of dead muscle to replacement by scar, and the scar does not contract. Then yellowish and soft with a red border, necrotic tissue removed by one to two weeks, tough fibrous scar by six. — 6. Cardiac Pathophysiology for posting.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"What happens to left ventricular stroke volume in mitral stenosis?","choices":["It decreases","It increases to compensate","It is unchanged until the valve closes completely","It becomes dependent on atrial contraction alone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The ventricle is not diseased; it is simply not being filled, which is why the problem is upstream of it. — 6. Cardiac Pathophysiology for posting.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"At what point does myocardium become ischaemic?","choices":["When metabolic demand for oxygen exceeds supply","When coronary blood flow falls below a fixed absolute value","Only once a coronary artery is completely occluded","Only when the patient experiences chest pain"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It is a relationship rather than a threshold, which is why ischaemia can arise either from supply falling or from demand rising with the supply unchanged. — 6. Cardiac Pathophysiology for posting.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"What is the initiating feature of myocardial infarction?","choices":["Gradual narrowing of the lumen over years","Vasospasm of an angiographically normal artery","Inflammation of the pericardium","Thrombosis on an ulcerated or cracked atherosclerotic plaque"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The plaque has to fail before the thrombus forms, which is why plaque stability matters more than plaque size. — 6. Cardiac Pathophysiology for posting.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"Why does slowly progressive plaque formation allow the heart to tolerate more narrowing?","choices":["The myocardium reduces its oxygen demand permanently","Collateral circulation develops over the years the plaque is growing","The plaque itself becomes permeable to blood","Cardiac cells switch to anaerobic metabolism indefinitely"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Time is what permits the adaptation, which is also why an abrupt occlusion in a previously healthy vessel can do more damage than a severe chronic stenosis. — 6. Cardiac Pathophysiology for posting.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"How does constrictive pericarditis impair the heart?","choices":["The sac fills rapidly with fluid, compressing the chambers","The heart adheres to surrounding mediastinal structures","The sac becomes dense","The myocardium becomes fibrotic and non-compliant"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and the result resembles restrictive cardiomyopathy in that filling is what fails -- but the stiffness here is outside the muscle rather than within it. Non-elastic and scarred, encasing the heart and impairing diastolic filling. — 6. Cardiac Pathophysiology for posting.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"On what does the result of vessel occlusion depend?","choices":["Collateral circulation","The lipoprotein pattern of the patient","Which valve is nearest the occluded segment","The presence of pericardial fluid"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The same occlusion in two hearts produces different damage, which is why the vessel alone does not predict the outcome. Workload and the length of time. — 6. Cardiac Pathophysiology for posting.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"What are the consequences of myocardial ischaemia?","choices":["Abnormal rhythm only, with function preserved throughout","Abnormal heart function and abnormal rhythm","Immediate irreversible damage in every case","Valvular incompetence as the first consequence"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Two immediate consequences and one that depends on duration, which is why time is the variable that decides how much muscle is lost. With irreversible damage if prolonged. — 6. Cardiac Pathophysiology for posting.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"What is the difference between valvular stenosis and regurgitation?","choices":["Stenosis is failure to close, regurgitation is failure to open","Both describe failure of the valve to open","Stenosis is failure to open completely, regurgitation is failure to close completely","Stenosis affects only the mitral valve and regurgitation only the aortic"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and the consequence follows directly: one makes the heart push harder, the other makes it push more. — 6. Cardiac Pathophysiology for posting.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"A serosanguinous pericardial effusion is secondary to which causes?","choices":["Heart failure and hypoproteinaemia together","Blunt trauma, cardiac surgery or resuscitation","Obstruction to lymphatic drainage alone","Penetrating cardiac trauma only"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. All three involve mechanical force on the chest, which is what puts blood into what would otherwise be a serous fluid. — 6. Cardiac Pathophysiology for posting.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"Why does atrial fibrillation develop in mitral stenosis?","choices":["The ventricle hypertrophies and compresses the conducting system","Coronary ischaemia damages the sinus node","Pericardial inflammation spreads to the atrium","The atrium enlarges as its volume rises"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and the enlarged fibrillating atrium then predisposes to clot within it, which is the next consequence in the chain. — 6. Cardiac Pathophysiology for posting.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"How do the chambers compensate in mitral regurgitation?","choices":["The ventricle hypertrophies without dilating","The atrium shrinks to limit the regurgitant volume","Both atrium and ventricle dilate and hypertrophy to handle the extra volume","Neither chamber changes until failure occurs"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Volume work produces dilation alongside hypertrophy, as against the hypertrophy alone that pressure work produces. — 6. Cardiac Pathophysiology for posting.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"Which factors are described as impairing coronary perfusion?","choices":["Plaques, thrombosis, vasospasm, microcirculation, pressure","Increased heart rate and contractility, acting alone","Valvular stenosis and regurgitation, acting alone","Pericardial inflammation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Five different ways to arrive at the same shortfall, only one of which is the fixed narrowing people picture first. — 6. Cardiac Pathophysiology for posting.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"Which are given as causes of the initiating endothelial injury?","choices":["Wall stress, toxins, inflammation, hyperlipidaemia","Valvular calcification and rheumatic scarring","Viral infection of the myocardium","Pericardial inflammation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Mechanical, chemical, inflammatory and metabolic insults all converge on the same first step, which is why so many different risk factors end in the same lesion. — 6. Cardiac Pathophysiology for posting.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Atherosclerosis narrows the coronary lumen. Which processes does that narrowing predispose to?","choices":["Valvular calcification and regurgitation","Pericardial effusion and tamponade","Myocardial leukocyte infiltration and necrosis without vessel involvement","Thrombosis, coronary vasospasm and endothelial cell dysfunction"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The narrowing is the setting rather than the whole disease: what precipitates an event is one of these three acting on an already narrowed vessel. — 6. Cardiac Pathophysiology for posting.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"At what degree of closure does stenosis begin to affect haemodynamics?","choices":["About ten per cent","About fifty per cent","About seventy-five per cent","Only at complete closure"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. It is a lower threshold than the seventy-five per cent that compromises coronary flow, and the two figures are easy to swap. — 6. Cardiac Pathophysiology for posting.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"What determines the risk that a plaque will rupture?","choices":["Its age alone","Its composition","The degree of luminal narrowing alone","The lipoprotein that delivered the lipid"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Two factors, one internal and one external, which is why an identical plaque may be stable in one vessel and not in another. The mechanical stresses acting on it. — 6. Cardiac Pathophysiology for posting.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"What initiates atherosclerotic plaque formation?","choices":["Deposition of calcium on the intimal surface","Rupture of the vasa vasorum within the arterial wall","Platelet aggregation on an intact endothelium","Injury to the endothelial cells of the vessel"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Everything that follows -- permeability, leukocytes, oxidised lipid, foam cells -- is downstream of that first injury. — 6. Cardiac Pathophysiology for posting.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Which organisms most commonly cause infective endocarditis?","choices":["Streptococcal strains and Staphylococcus aureus","Coxsackievirus and adenovirus","Candida alone","Group A streptococcus through immune cross-reactivity"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The same two names recur, with the less virulent organisms reserved for the subacute form on an already damaged valve. — 6. Cardiac Pathophysiology for posting.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"Which are listed as PROBABLE rather than major risk factors?","choices":["Hypertension, diabetes and obesity","Age and family history","Abnormal lipids and cigarette smoking","Male sex, homocysteine and high sensitivity C reactive protein"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Two of the three are laboratory measurements rather than clinical facts, which is part of why they sit in the weaker category. — 6. Cardiac Pathophysiology for posting.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"Why does a high afterload worsen mitral regurgitation?","choices":["It reduces left atrial pressure","It prevents the ventricle from dilating","It increases the amount of blood regurgitated","It converts the regurgitation into stenosis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The ventricle takes the path of least resistance, so the harder it is to eject forwards, the more goes backwards. — 6. Cardiac Pathophysiology for posting.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"What distinguishes angina pectoris from infarction at the cellular level?","choices":["The ischaemia affects only the right ventricle","There is no ischaemia, only abnormal conduction","The ischaemia is intermittent and insufficient to kill cardiac cells","Cell death occurs but is confined to the endocardium"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Same mechanism, different endpoint: the supply falls short but recovers before the cells are lost. — 6. Cardiac Pathophysiology for posting.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"What is restrictive cardiomyopathy most often related to?","choices":["Alcohol and pregnancy","A specific clinical condition","Coxsackievirus infection","Ageing valvular calcification"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. It is usually secondary to something identifiable, unlike the other two patterns which are more often idiopathic. Such as amyloidosis. — 6. Cardiac Pathophysiology for posting.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"Which effusion type follows heart failure or hypoproteinaemia?","choices":["A chylous collection","A haemopericardium","A serosanguinous effusion","A serous transudate"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Both causes act by altering the forces governing fluid movement rather than by injuring anything. — 6. Cardiac Pathophysiology for posting.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"What is the haemodynamic consequence of aortic stenosis?","choices":["Obstruction to outflow during systole","Obstruction to filling during diastole","Backflow into the ventricle during diastole","Backflow into the atrium during systole"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The aortic valve is open during systole, so this is when the obstruction bites. With a pressure gradient across the valve during ejection. — 6. Cardiac Pathophysiology for posting.pptx, Slide 51"}]};
